1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,178
A workflow uses application-defined control flow.

2
00:00:03,328 --> 00:00:08,601
Common patterns include a sequence of transformations, routing by request type, parallel

3
00:00:08,601 --> 00:00:12,117
independent lookups, and a review step before completion.

4
00:00:12,267 --> 00:00:16,245
An agent can be placed inside one stage rather than controlling the

5
00:00:16,245 --> 00:00:16,908
entire system.

6
00:00:17,058 --> 00:00:21,294
This limits the scope of uncertainty and makes recovery easier to explain.

7
00:00:21,444 --> 00:00:25,334
Parallelize independent reads only when they improve end-to-end latency.

8
00:00:25,484 --> 00:00:30,065
Limit concurrency with a semaphore or executor capacity, and give every branch

9
00:00:30,065 --> 00:00:30,829
a deadline.

10
00:00:30,979 --> 00:00:35,729
Do not concurrently execute dependent actions such as reserving stock before validating

11
00:00:35,729 --> 00:00:36,521
the product.

12
00:00:36,671 --> 00:00:41,243
A failed optional lookup can produce a partial answer; a failed mandatory

13
00:00:41,243 --> 00:00:43,529
authorization check must stop the operation.

14
00:00:43,679 --> 00:00:48,085
A support workflow first validates the signed-in user, then reads an order

15
00:00:48,085 --> 00:00:51,757
and a public policy in parallel, then generates an explanation.

16
00:00:51,907 --> 00:00:54,817
Refund execution remains outside this workflow.

17
00:00:54,967 --> 00:00:59,614
If policy retrieval fails, the system may report the order status while

18
00:00:59,614 --> 00:01:02,325
clearly saying the next-step policy is unavailable.

19
00:01:02,475 --> 00:01:04,678
It must not silently assume approval.

20
00:01:04,828 --> 00:01:08,958
Implement two bounded read tasks using Java 21 virtual threads.

21
00:01:09,108 --> 00:01:12,456
Give one task a delayed response and record total latency.

22
00:01:12,606 --> 00:01:15,516
Define which failures permit a partial response.

23
00:01:15,666 --> 00:01:19,431
Use a deadline shared by both futures and cancel unfinished work when

24
00:01:19,431 --> 00:01:21,313
the response is no longer useful.

25
00:01:21,463 --> 00:01:24,316
Restore interrupt status when handling interruption.

26
00:01:24,466 --> 00:01:28,522
Limit outbound concurrency independently of the number of virtual threads.

27
00:01:28,672 --> 00:01:33,181
A test should verify the partial-result contract, not only that two tasks

28
00:01:33,181 --> 00:01:33,557
started.

29
00:01:33,707 --> 00:01:34,378
Question.

30
00:01:34,528 --> 00:01:36,318
When is parallelization wrong?

31
00:01:36,468 --> 00:01:37,045
Answer.

32
00:01:37,195 --> 00:01:42,231
When steps depend on prior results, share an unsafe resource, or overload

33
00:01:42,231 --> 00:01:43,490
a constrained dependency.

34
00:01:43,640 --> 00:01:44,311
Question.

35
00:01:44,461 --> 00:01:47,138
Does adding more agents guarantee a better answer?

36
00:01:47,288 --> 00:01:47,864
Answer.

37
00:01:48,014 --> 00:01:48,498
No.

38
00:01:48,648 --> 00:01:54,882
It adds coordination, latency, and correlated error opportunities; compare against a simpler

39
00:01:54,882 --> 00:01:55,402
baseline.
